    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 1091,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153550,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 1091,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T15:08:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1076,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 1092,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153551,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 1092,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients typically present with nonspecific symptoms and signs related to a pelvic/abdominal mass, although some cases are discovered incidentally on imaging or following oophorectomy {2032767}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T15:08:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1077,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 1093,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153552,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 1093,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are rare. They typically occur in women of reproductive age {23722509; 2032767}; however, no population-based data have been published so far, and the epidemiology of the tumour remains largely unstudied.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T15:08:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1078,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 1094,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153553,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 1094,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T15:08:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1079,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 1095,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153554,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 1095,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pathogenesis is uncertain. It has been postulated that ovarian myxomas may be related to the fibroma&nbsp;/ thecoma&nbsp;/ sclerosing stromal tumour categories, because some of these ovarian stromal tumours exhibit small areas of myxomatous stroma resembling myxomas {7688213; 25083960}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T15:08:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1086,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 1102,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153555,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 1102,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours range in size but are typically &lt;&nbsp;100&nbsp;mm and show a mucoid/gelatinous cut surface, sometimes punctated by cysts {2032767}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T15:08:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1080,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 1096,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153556,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 1096,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Myxomas are intraparenchymal and are typically well circumscribed, although not encapsulated. They have abundant background myxoid matrix that displaces follicles and their derivative structures. Cellularity varies from area to area, with ovarian myxomas often being more cellular than their intramuscular counterparts. Spindled to stellate cells are haphazardly arranged and display long, tapering cytoplasmic processes and oval, sometimes indented, bland nuclei with occasional nuclear pseudoinclusions and absent to minimal mitotic activity. Small capillary-type vessels, sometimes with a plexiform arrangement, are typically seen throughout the tumour. Small cysts/pseudocysts and areas of haemorrhage with or without fibrin deposition may be seen.</p>\r\n<p>Ovarian myxomas typically show positive staining with Alcian blue because of the abundant hyaluronic acid in their myxomatous stroma {28840847}. On immunohistochemistry, the tumour cells are typically positive for vimentin and SMA, but they are negative for inhibin, desmin, S100, cytokeratins, and EMA {1996543; 7688213; 2032767}. By electron microscopy, the tumour cells show fibroblastic/myofibroblastic differentiation {1502739}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T15:08:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1081,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 1097,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 153557,
        "bookId": 85,
        "chapterId": 186,
        "headingId": 1097,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-08-26T15:08:46"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 1082,
    "bookid": 85,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
